#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166558 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a6ca425-0e94-5b83-8729-fda13fbf3d7d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "aeb0ab38-6a30-586d-bcb4-8d3c6b8c9d9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8409" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm  d t} + y + 6 = y^2.$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=-3$\n\n- $y=-2$\n\n- $y=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation $y'(t) = f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm  d t} = y^2 - y - 6,\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y) = y^2 - y - 6.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=-3$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(-3) = (-3)^2 - (-3) - 6 = 6 \\neq 0.$  $\\quad \\color{red}{\\times}$\n\n- $y = -2$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(-2) = (-2)^2 - (-2) - 6 = 0.$$\\quad  \\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n\n- $y= -1$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(-1) = (-1)^2 - (-1) - 6 = -4 \\neq 0.$ $\\quad \\color{red}{\\times}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166559 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "499fe0bc-77df-53df-8493-7d4c107031d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "ccdc8d9f-641a-593f-8999-ea02e14b3ed3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8410" :question/problem "Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm  d x}= y^2+4y.$" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation $y'(x) = f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that $f(c)=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)= y^2+4y,$ so we solve the equation $f(c)=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =0 \\\\ {c}^{2}+4c & =0 \\\\ c(c+4) & =0 \\\\ c & =-4,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y_1(x)=-4$ and $y_2(x)=0$ are the equilibrium solutions of the given differential equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166560 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba6a29e6-4dab-5854-815c-fa26292c31d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Equilibrium Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "eaeec2ed-cb61-5887-94a8-b7ab30aa5d1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8411" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm  d x}= y^3-3y^2-6y+8.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y_1(x)=4$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n\n- The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions, $y_1(x), y_2(x),$ and $y_3(x)$\n\n- $y_1(x) + y_2(x)+y_3(x) =-3$" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation $y'(x) = f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)=y^3-3y^2-6y+8.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true.  We have that $f(4)=4^3-3(4)^2-6(4)+8 = 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. We know that $y=4$ is a solution to $f(y) = 0,$ and so $(y-4)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y-4)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | $y^3$ | $y^2$ | $y^1$ | $y^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-3$ | $-6$ | $8$ |\n|  | $\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$ | $4$ | $4$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y-4)({y}^{2}+y-2) \\\\  & =(y-4)(y-1)(y+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has three distinct equilibrium solutions, \n\n$$\ny_1(x) =4, \\quad y_2(x) = 1, \\quad y_3(x) = -2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have $y_1(x)+y_2(x) + y_3(x) = 4+1+(-2) = 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a6ca425-0e94-5b83-8729-fda13fbf3d7d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c3c2a28-0b6c-5ae8-a124-8a082c07c13a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307392} {:answer/id #uuid "ea527680-a145-5edf-8a6f-9b01dc8d7cba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307393} {:answer/id #uuid "98a5501b-5826-5c2d-a265-345b27c00842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307394} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e8ec0d-5c42-5c82-9bd5-a50232cf8f90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307395} {:answer/id #uuid "467c800a-fc1c-5411-8ecd-a08b8c80ec1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9e8ec0d-5c42-5c82-9bd5-a50232cf8f90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307395} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ea9e8ce-313c-5433-8acc-cfa8fedbe7bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "539f5aea-e270-503e-8d41-9c9f4899cc73" :question/math-academy-id "q-160419" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\pi .$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=0$\n- $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n- $y=\\pi $" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y+\\pi ,\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y+\\pi .$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=0$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(0)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-0+\\pi =\\pi \\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi =1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=\\pi $ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\pi +\\pi =0-\\pi +\\pi =0.$$\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a6ca425-0e94-5b83-8729-fda13fbf3d7d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d5afb17-8908-5496-9ecc-3733ebdb60df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307399} {:answer/id #uuid "7dad0ed2-0467-5bd5-bd80-cc3639bba3e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307400} {:answer/id #uuid "9e814941-5333-5c01-9d68-36e66d48917f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307401} {:answer/id #uuid "9734ad6c-6773-5879-93d6-5ed3cdb7116d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307402} {:answer/id #uuid "1aa9d4e0-9c68-5ea2-a794-2e89d26dd421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9734ad6c-6773-5879-93d6-5ed3cdb7116d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307402} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ebb1e5d-15a7-576b-b154-41aa9146354b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "480185ab-957a-5a1f-816b-f2661a017908" :question/math-academy-id "q-160422" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}=1.$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=-1$\n- $y=1$\n- $y=2$" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)=1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=-1$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(-1)=1-\\frac{1}{(-1)^{2}}=1-1=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=1$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(1)=1-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}=1-1=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=2$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(2)=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{4}\\ne 0.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "499fe0bc-77df-53df-8493-7d4c107031d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "517a7dcb-9be3-55ad-977f-8ff52da46471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The equation has no equilibrium solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307406} {:answer/id #uuid "df7c1cad-d962-57e2-a24c-2cc744293426" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y(x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307407} {:answer/id #uuid "86372fcd-aec3-54f3-8bd8-9875c840e411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y(x)=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307408} {:answer/id #uuid "99dde965-4446-559f-8858-1b0d9f68b553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=-\\pi $ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307409} {:answer/id #uuid "ad8015a8-613e-5425-8732-cafd11a3137b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=0$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "517a7dcb-9be3-55ad-977f-8ff52da46471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The equation has no equilibrium solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307406} :answer-field/id #uuid "83d6f203-e811-5bb1-b7d6-fd9d30ba4328" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a785f16-be14-5d27-b10a-d2d350268268" :question/math-academy-id "q-160429" :question/problem "Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2.$" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that $f(c)=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2,$ so we attempt solve the equation $f(c)=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡c+2 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡c & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, note that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡c|\\le 1$ for all $c.$ Therefore, the above equation has no (real) solutions.\n\nTherefore, the differential equation has no equilibrium points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "499fe0bc-77df-53df-8493-7d4c107031d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c67e3bd3-b773-57eb-ac68-a5a4028d0b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y(x)=-\\sqrt[4]{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307413} {:answer/id #uuid "9e6cbea7-c4f5-5941-baee-f034502f319e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=-\\sqrt[4]{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt[4]{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307414} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c4df28-b16d-5f2f-a456-9f5c35ef349f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The equation has no equilibrium solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307415} {:answer/id #uuid "aad7e142-bfc2-580e-aa01-ae72dba1e9dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y(x)=\\sqrt[4]{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307416} {:answer/id #uuid "8b4d30b0-3a6e-5abe-9117-c474663154d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=-\\sqrt[4]{2},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=0,\\,{y}_{3}(x)=1$ and ${y}_{4}(x)=\\sqrt[4]{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8c4df28-b16d-5f2f-a456-9f5c35ef349f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The equation has no equilibrium solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307415} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7f27ba6-755e-5358-afc6-678f14195e98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "60c5789b-2351-59f0-befd-5e964082145c" :question/math-academy-id "q-160432" :question/problem "Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{{y}^{4}+2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that $f(c)=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)=\\sqrt{{y}^{4}+2},$ so we attempt solve the equation $f(c)=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{c}^{4}+2} & =0 \\\\ {c}^{4}+2 & =0 \\\\ {c}^{4} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, note that ${c}^{4}\\ge 0$ for all $c.$ Therefore, the above equation has no (real) solutions.\n\nTherefore, the differential equation has no equilibrium points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba6a29e6-4dab-5854-815c-fa26292c31d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Equilibrium Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcc12c51-ee79-58d2-877c-7faad905b73f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307420} {:answer/id #uuid "c0931547-c610-536e-b878-0a51ca26d72c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307421} {:answer/id #uuid "38b5b4c7-9730-56bc-86f6-6204aa6f2013" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307422} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f4068b-3588-550c-ab05-134d81aa474a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307423} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a2abc9-ce78-5fc4-a34f-3b3e82f3a9dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38b5b4c7-9730-56bc-86f6-6204aa6f2013" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307422} :answer-field/id #uuid "b562362d-d7ce-506e-ae1c-000c72790521" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "311821bf-0263-5c25-b2ce-74326edbc417" :question/math-academy-id "q-160444" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+3y+1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y(t)=-1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- $y(t)=1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+3y+1.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have that $f(-1)=(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}+3(-1)+1=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have that $f(1)={1}^{3}+3(1)^{2}+3(1)+1=8\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We know that $y=-1$ is a solution to $f(y)=0,$ and so $(y+1)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y+1)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | ${y}^{3}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | ${y}^{1}$ | ${y}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y+1)({y}^{2}+2y+1) \\\\  & =(y+1)(y+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(y+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has exactly one equilibrium solution,\n\n$$\ny(t)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "499fe0bc-77df-53df-8493-7d4c107031d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfda458c-a787-5a30-aa60-2a81fd3d893d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307427} {:answer/id #uuid "d42f5ca5-8dd5-57f0-af08-0c6c19254fbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d42f5ca5-8dd5-57f0-af08-0c6c19254fbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307428} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bf9b2ad-bf8a-53b4-98d1-a8ac631590f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba4da62a-ca77-514c-94e0-a8cc65753608" :question/math-academy-id "q-352876" :question/problem "Find the equilibrium solution $y(x)$ of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-7)^{2}.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that $f(c)=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)=(y-7)^{2},$ so we attempt solve the equation $f(c)=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =0 \\\\ (c-7)^{2} & =0 \\\\ c-7 & =0 \\\\ c & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y(x)=7$ is the only equilibrium solution of the given differential equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "499fe0bc-77df-53df-8493-7d4c107031d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9aa54b8-3b41-5561-96cf-f3783de33add" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9aa54b8-3b41-5561-96cf-f3783de33add" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307431} :answer-field/id #uuid "71b5b1dc-22bd-5f6a-b98b-dc1103e26d48" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307430} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6b8f1d3-5d32-5886-a0b9-01dcdcbb15ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6b8f1d3-5d32-5886-a0b9-01dcdcbb15ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307433} :answer-field/id #uuid "de7813f4-383e-5ced-bbb9-3435a37632ef" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "882b2bbe-3cb6-52dd-ac03-52548104d807" :question/math-academy-id "q-352884" :question/problem "**In ascending order**, the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{2}-2y-15$\nare\n\n${y}_{1}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}(t)=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that $f(c)=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{2}-2y-15,$ so we solve the equation $f(c)=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =0 \\\\ {c}^{2}-2c-15 & =0 \\\\ (c-5)(c+3) & =0 \\\\ c & =-3,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, ${y}_{1}(t)=-3$ and ${y}_{2}(t)=5$ are the equilibrium solutions of the given differential equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a6ca425-0e94-5b83-8729-fda13fbf3d7d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cedd6ab1-7671-57fb-b70e-996d90c05c1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165511} {:answer/id #uuid "ceed2781-70a9-54b2-b622-28c6aee27867" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165512} {:answer/id #uuid "0086c7e7-1a85-56ef-899b-8e5a752d0b14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165513} {:answer/id #uuid "11111cd8-4b0d-58b3-8c07-9d40d47fb18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165514} {:answer/id #uuid "31dd359e-b5b5-5b54-aaff-df55a14c35f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31dd359e-b5b5-5b54-aaff-df55a14c35f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165515} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d827717-c0df-59ff-b5c4-582a47736264" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73083626-cc38-5982-9c18-9459efa65b4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-95572" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{y}^{2}=2y+{y}^{3}.$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=0$\n- $y=1$\n- $y=2$" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{3}+{y}^{2}+2y,\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)={y}^{3}+{y}^{2}+2y.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=0$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(0)={0}^{3}+{0}^{2}+2(0)=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=1$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(1)={1}^{3}+{1}^{2}+2(1)=4\\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=2$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(2)={2}^{3}+{2}^{2}+2(2)=16\\ne 0.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba6a29e6-4dab-5854-815c-fa26292c31d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Equilibrium Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d22ef741-508b-54c5-811e-43007fcdf7cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165571} {:answer/id #uuid "09541b96-fc29-5e76-887f-36c95d134c00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165572} {:answer/id #uuid "15227065-3083-5026-93e5-afdd3a890e33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165573} {:answer/id #uuid "580099cd-75db-59a5-a6fe-9b291d7d964a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165574} {:answer/id #uuid "7281a96e-024d-54b4-a3ab-238627351e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d22ef741-508b-54c5-811e-43007fcdf7cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165571} :answer-field/id #uuid "a55d6467-1dae-5884-a30b-1d80ab5ac367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff7b1af7-af38-533c-a6d2-a2ca545a8e42" :question/math-academy-id "q-95733" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{3}-7y+6.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions, ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and ${y}_{3}(x)$\n- ${y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)+{y}_{3}(x)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{3}-7y+6.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true.  We have that $f(1)={1}^{3}-7(1)+6=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. We know that $y=1$ is a solution to $f(y)=0,$ and so $(y-1)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y-1)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | ${y}^{3}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | ${y}^{1}$ | ${y}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y-1)({y}^{2}+y-6) \\\\  & =(y-1)(y-2)(y+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has three distinct equilibrium solutions, \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=1,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=2,\\,{y}_{3}(x)=-3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have ${y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)+{y}_{3}(x)=1+2+(-3)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a6ca425-0e94-5b83-8729-fda13fbf3d7d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac0f3f1f-b11f-5367-a0ce-008e1cab3abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307436} {:answer/id #uuid "8886f4c9-405b-5fc0-a5dc-77793193c184" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307437} {:answer/id #uuid "c2063b9c-47c7-5ab2-9c23-dcf26525752a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307438} {:answer/id #uuid "5d400e8a-6871-58a1-ae8e-92ae72ab9ad5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307439} {:answer/id #uuid "ffab8a53-097c-5994-88fc-1e9dc9bfb887" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2063b9c-47c7-5ab2-9c23-dcf26525752a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307438} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8471307-6024-55da-baf1-2c8c43bbe939" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7fa112f-07d0-55e0-a7a4-18de9a5a287d" :question/math-academy-id "q-96070" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+y={y}^{2}-2.$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=2$\n- $y=1$\n- $y=4$" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{2}-y-2,\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)={y}^{2}-y-2.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=2$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(2)={2}^{2}-2-2=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=1$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(1)={1}^{2}-1-2=-2\\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=4$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(4)={4}^{2}-4-2=10\\ne 0.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a6ca425-0e94-5b83-8729-fda13fbf3d7d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ae1623f-993c-52c1-a93a-7f56da345322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307443} {:answer/id #uuid "835094d4-de03-56d8-be93-f2dc08f155f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307444} {:answer/id #uuid "2e830bf7-8ee9-5078-b729-f3a169db3a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307445} {:answer/id #uuid "77e0a403-05b3-5bce-9ea1-8d7bb5f4de87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307446} {:answer/id #uuid "833dc14b-3f63-5386-8308-aea2077dd160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "833dc14b-3f63-5386-8308-aea2077dd160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307447} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9dd7a4c-1cc7-5eb6-8a4e-7db340840978" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f055d4d5-5380-57eb-a27f-3b91f629b3bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-96072" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{2}={y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=1$\n- $y=e$\n- $y=2$" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-{e}^{2},\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-{e}^{2}.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=1$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(1)=(1)^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-{e}^{2}=-{e}^{2}\\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=e$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(e)={e}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-{e}^{2}={e}^{2}-{e}^{2}=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=2$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(2)={2}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-{e}^{2}\\ne 0.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "499fe0bc-77df-53df-8493-7d4c107031d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb3c5315-0f06-5f4b-8850-479f72682ca8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=-6$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307450} {:answer/id #uuid "870a9709-a298-53cd-8ed5-d6a4fe0620a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=0$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307451} {:answer/id #uuid "e93a18f6-875f-5686-a86e-3517e0d36260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307452} {:answer/id #uuid "05b97d97-7d4c-51eb-a6dc-ff9b23e33d15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307453} {:answer/id #uuid "179488ee-017d-5d74-b45a-8ecf76a4b479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "179488ee-017d-5d74-b45a-8ecf76a4b479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307454} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeb3b0d8-ac57-5640-8baf-5fc9b36cc574" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ed080b9-cedf-52eb-a342-0c49d7416363" :question/math-academy-id "q-96159" :question/problem "Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-6y+9.$" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that $f(c)=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{2}-6y+9,$ so we attempt solve the equation $f(c)=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =0 \\\\ {c}^{2}-6c+9 & =0 \\\\ (c-3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ c-3 & =0 \\\\ c & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{1}(x)=3$  is the only equilibrium solution of the given differential equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba6a29e6-4dab-5854-815c-fa26292c31d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Equilibrium Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5793135a-3e27-5572-9818-332f9eb36d36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307457} {:answer/id #uuid "edadb0a5-e119-59a6-a71a-d0ab8a5b824c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307458} {:answer/id #uuid "a15318a6-254d-515d-ad7d-4eda692b69ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307459} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0e4cb1-3a0a-5a1f-9553-2899cd15d141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307460} {:answer/id #uuid "2c7db085-0016-53e9-9543-3ff90358c687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a15318a6-254d-515d-ad7d-4eda692b69ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307459} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcd9c747-c267-58a2-905c-9844ba7f534c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2c60a39c-067b-5ec0-8cd6-367f8fd5b1e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-96174" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{3}-7{y}^{2}+15y-9.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=3$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- $y=-1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- The equation has $2$ distinct equilibrium solutions" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is a equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{3}-7{y}^{2}+15y-9.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true.  We have that $f(3)=(3)^{3}-7(3)^{2}+15(3)-9=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false.  We have that $f(-1)=(-1)^{3}-7(-1)^{2}+15(-1)-9=-32\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We know that $y=3$ is a solution to $f(y)=0,$ and so $(y-3)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y-3)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | ${y}^{3}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | ${y}^{1}$ | ${y}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-7$ | $15$ | $-9$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $-12$ | $9$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-4$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y-3)({y}^{2}-4y+3) \\\\  & =(y-3)(y-1)(y-3) \\\\  & =(y-1)(y-3)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has $2$ distinct equilibrium solutions, \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(t)=1,\\,{y}_{2}(t)=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba6a29e6-4dab-5854-815c-fa26292c31d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Equilibrium Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab60e6c-bea9-5f2f-bb02-461b6337a953" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307464} {:answer/id #uuid "be4ba1fa-12b3-5be1-baaf-7d62c87ea971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307465} {:answer/id #uuid "b2f16027-1775-5bba-9c45-9d3fb347b4ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307466} {:answer/id #uuid "eba455c3-b642-552d-abf1-40f09ee229d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307467} {:answer/id #uuid "87f6630a-29c8-58c8-8722-1138fcd84a40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2f16027-1775-5bba-9c45-9d3fb347b4ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307466} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0731521-4ddb-5ad4-914b-cf8cce8234e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8a2030de-b3d6-5544-b87f-48497f01c155" :question/math-academy-id "q-96175" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}+12y+8.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=-2$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- $y=2$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}+12y+8.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have that $f(-2)=(-2)^{3}+6(-2)^{2}+12(-2)+8=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have that $f(2)=(2)^{3}+6(2)^{2}+12(2)+8=64\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We know that $y=-2$ is a solution to $f(y)=0,$ and so $(y+2)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y+2)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | ${y}^{3}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | ${y}^{1}$ | ${y}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $1$ | $6$ | $12$ | $8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-2$ | $-8$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $4$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y+2)({y}^{2}+4y+4) \\\\  & =(y+2)(y+2)^{2} \\\\  & =(y+2)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has exactly one equilibrium solution,\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(t)=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307469 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba6a29e6-4dab-5854-815c-fa26292c31d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Equilibrium Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbd9d3ff-4ae6-5fa3-97e9-d7bf18fe7e2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307471} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb91607-f495-5b78-b92f-9cfd810b214d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307472} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec708ed-60fe-5872-bc5e-2263713b92b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307473} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9ca367-22a6-5231-9683-76593d2272d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307474} {:answer/id #uuid "010971a2-8a62-5585-b32e-5da2ad6b6539" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "010971a2-8a62-5585-b32e-5da2ad6b6539" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307475} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c6ee5c9-1414-5823-a0de-9e2b63cfa7cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9b4d553c-e299-55ea-b4d3-be84f30df0df" :question/math-academy-id "q-96176" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}+9y+4.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- $y=-4$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}+9y+4.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false.  We have that $f(1)=(1)^{3}+6(1)^{2}+9(1)+4=20\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true.  We have that $f(-4)=(-4)^{3}+6(-4)^{2}+9(-4)+4=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We know that $y=-4$ is a solution to $f(y)=0,$ and so $(y+4)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y+4)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | ${y}^{3}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | ${y}^{1}$ | ${y}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $1$ | $6$ | $9$ | $4$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-4$ | $-8$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y+4)({y}^{2}+2y+1) \\\\  & =(y+4)(y+1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has $2$ distinct equilibrium solutions, \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(t)=-4,\\,{y}_{2}(t)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
